{"title":"2013 Indonesia MO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2013_Indonesia_MO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be a quadratic (polynomial of degree two) with a positive leading coefficient and negative discriminant. Prove that there exists three quadratics <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/d/3fd628c2338dc339d3e6a10257752d208924b1eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1, P_2, P_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" /> such that:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/2/782014c34705c55ebcb06b28ddebee6a32a85eac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x) = P_1(x) + P_2(x) + P_3(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"237\" height=\"18\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/d/3fd628c2338dc339d3e6a10257752d208924b1eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1, P_2, P_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" /> have positive leading coefficients and zero discriminants (and hence each has a double root)</li>\n<li>The roots of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/d/3fd628c2338dc339d3e6a10257752d208924b1eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1, P_2, P_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" /> are different</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2013_Indonesia_MO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/7/4f75c38e0a961969f1bc5d39e2b487dcf9ac9700.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i = 1,2,3, P_i(x) = \\frac{1}{3}a(x+r_i)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"240\" height=\"38\" />, where the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/c/96c42077f2a447ca548cdbfe63f1932e9866df3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />'s are all distinct. If we can choose pairwise distinct <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/c/96c42077f2a447ca548cdbfe63f1932e9866df3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/c/afc6850e31359049d081e2e674c1abe77fa3a9d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=1}^3 r_i = \\frac{3b}{2a}, \\sum_{i=1}^3 r_i^2 = \\frac{3c}{a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"189\" height=\"55\" />, we are done, because clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/b/6eba82ad77a3eddeff007a3f85396a159447b0f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a}{3} &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"49\" height=\"34\" />, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e86919caff4a26af2c8867c56bcd9134bfd241ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />'s only have one distinct real root, and\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/4/0c43da68a3c0db61ad3e26f60e8bfdd074ae1a27.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[b^2 - 4ac = \\left(\\frac{2a}{3}(r_1 + r_2 + r_3)\\right)^2 - \\frac{4}{3}a^2(r_1^2 + r_2^2 + r_3^2) =\\frac{4a^2}{3}\\left(\\frac{(r_1 + r_2 + r_3)^2}{3} - (r_1^2 + r_2^2 + r_3^2)\\right) &lt; 0\\]\" width=\"739\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>by AM.GM, where the inequality is strict because equality when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c2146c9bab9b0fd1e9f1ab2c79c0ebfaaadd64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 = r_2 = r_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"13\" /> cannot hold. It now suffices to show we can indeed choose pairwise distinct <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/c/96c42077f2a447ca548cdbfe63f1932e9866df3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />'s satisfying the equations above.\n</p><p>Consider the polynomial with roots <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/2/ed2b055c89a98960f69ada4b7990044d435b27b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1, r_2, r_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"11\" /> of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/5/9854d3021ce272946430bd2652b4d6ee8ff2fe60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = x^3 - \\frac{3b}{2a}x^2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{3b}{2a}\\right)^2 - \\frac{3c}{a}\\right)x + K = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"394\" height=\"55\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b9a03f300423c2c729f03791d8cbc82e24a8abe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K = -r_1r_2r_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"104\" height=\"17\" />. For three distinct roots, we must have exactly two turning points. We see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/d/dfd782cb3176ac649ee5ed90ed255989b9c89a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f&#39;(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"20\" />, yields, after some simplification, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/1/641a40224d8d74a4c1fafc4342f1fe60da23a20a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 - \\frac{b}{a}x + \\frac{1}{6}\\left(\\left(\\frac{3b}{2a}\\right)^2 - \\frac{3c}{a}\\right) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"265\" height=\"55\" />. The discriminant of the quadratic on the LHS is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/9/89912bfa2bc45833f618bd588a1096ec64f3a026.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right)^2 - \\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{3b}{2a}\\right)^2 - \\frac{3c}{a}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{2a^2}(b^2 - 4ac) &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"395\" height=\"55\" /> (the discriminant of P &lt; 0). Thus, we are always guaranteed to find two turning points. By suitably choosing our value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />, we can ensure there exists <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/2/ed2b055c89a98960f69ada4b7990044d435b27b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1, r_2, r_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"11\" /> satisfying our system of equations. We are done!\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017232730\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.012 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.027 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 81/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4532/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:37784:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017232730 and revision id 292074. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}